    "dismiss," since there's nothing to dismiss. Honestly, this is a due-date view wearing a notifications hat.
  - [1mB — Persisted notification inbox.[0m The evaluator writes [38;5;153mNotification[39m records ([38;5;153mid[39m, [38;5;153mtaskId[39m, [38;5;153mkind[39m, [38;5;153mfiredAt[39m, [38;5;153mreadAt[39m) next
    to the tasks; the UI is an inbox with an unread badge. Buys history, per-item read/dismiss, and room for other
    notification kinds later — and it's the shape a server push system would eventually need. Costs: all the real
    bookkeeping. Dedup so a reload doesn't re-fire everything, cleanup when a task is deleted or its due date moves,
    pruning so the store doesn't grow forever. That's where the bugs in this kind of feature actually live.
  - [1mC — Derived state, transient toasts, snooze on the task (recommended).[0m Computed like A, plus a toast when a task
    crosses into due or overdue while you're watching, plus a [38;5;153msnoozedUntil[39m field on the task so dismissal means
    something. No notification entity.

  I recommend [1mC[0m. It produces the thing you actually asked for — something that [3mtells[0m you — without buying B's inbox
  bookkeeping to get history and read-state that nobody has asked for yet. And all three share the same core: a pure
  [38;5;153mevaluate(tasks, now)[39m function. Keeping that pure and isolated is what makes B reachable later if you ever add push, so
  choosing C now doesn't paint us in.

  [1mTooling[0m

  There's no tooling in this repo at all, and it's cheapest to set up before any code exists. The reminder evaluator is
  a pure function of [38;5;153m(tasks, now)[39m — time-dependent logic that's miserable to verify by hand and trivial to unit test
  with an injected clock. That's the one I'd insist on.

